  "step": [
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1,
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "The question of how often to reseal your cabin's interior is a good one, and the answer isn't always a simple yearly directive. It really depends on a few factors, like how much wear and tear your interior experiences, the type of sealant used, and even the product manufacturer's recommendations. Generally, for interior log and wood surfaces, you might be looking at reapplications of stains and clear coats every 3 to 7 years. Some interior log cabin guides even suggest restaining around every 3 to 5 years. It's a good idea to keep an eye on your wood surfaces; if they're starting to look dull, show signs of wear, or the protective layer seems to be diminishing, it's probably time to think about resealing. Don't wait until the wood is completely unprotected! When you're considering resealing, it’s also crucial to make sure the new product is compatible with any existing coatings and that the surface is properly prepped. If you're unsure about the right timing or the best product for your cabin, especially with existing coatings or uneven wear, a professional can really help guide you to ensure a long-lasting, beautiful finish."
    }
  ],
